    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "sometimes i just sit in my living room and it feels like i'm watching a movie of someone else's life. i spent thirty years in the OR, every second mattered, every move had a purpose. now i just... wake up, drink coffee, and wait for the day to end. it's like the person i actually was died when i hung up the scrubs, and this thing left behind is just a ghost. it feels so pointless.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "It's as if the intense focus and sense of purpose you had as a surgeon to save lives has been replaced by a feeling of aimlessness and disconnection. The sudden shift can be overwhelming and leave you feeling lost. Reflecting on the life savings you made during your 30-year career as a surgeon can be a poignant reminder of your significant contributions and accomplishments. It's understandable that you might feel like a part of you is still in the OR, unable to adjust to the quieter pace of civilian life.",
